   {
    "title": "Tables, keys and grain in an analytics warehouse",
    "minutes": 7,
    "body": "A database stores data in tables that point at each other. Before you write a single query, you need to know what one row in each table means.\n\n## Tables, rows and columns\n\nA **relational database** holds data in **tables**. Each **row** is one record and each **column** is one attribute of that record, with a fixed data type (integer, text, date, numeric).\n\nThroughout this course you work as a data analyst at **Nestwell**, a fictional online home-goods retailer. Its warehouse has five tables:\n\n| table | one row is | rows in our sample |\n|---|---|---|\n| `customers` | one customer account | 8 |\n| `orders` | one order placed by a customer | 16 |\n| `order_items` | one product line inside an order | 21 |\n| `products` | one product in the catalog | 6 |\n| `web_sessions` | one visit to the website or app | 23 |\n\nThe sample is tiny on purpose, so you can check every result by eye. Real tables hold millions of rows, but the logic is identical.\n\n## Keys: how tables connect\n\n- A **primary key** (PK) uniquely identifies each row. `orders.order_id` is the PK of `orders`: no two orders share it, and it is never `NULL`.\n- A **foreign key** (FK) is a column that points at another table's PK. `orders.customer_id` points at `customers.customer_id`.\n- A **composite key** uses more than one column. In `order_items`, the pair `(order_id, product_id)` is the key: one order can contain many products, but each product appears once per order.\n\nHere is how a few rows line up:\n\n| order_id | customer_id | order_date | status | total_amount |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1001 | 1 | 2026-01-05 | completed | 78.00 |\n| 1002 | 2 | 2026-01-14 | completed | 35.00 |\n| 1003 | 1 | 2026-01-28 | completed | 24.00 |\n| 1004 | 3 | 2026-02-02 | cancelled | 48.00 |\n\n| order_id | product_id | quantity | unit_price |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1001 | 101 | 1 | 48.00 |\n| 1001 | 102 | 1 | 30.00 |\n| 1002 | 104 | 1 | 35.00 |\n| 1003 | 103 | 2 | 12.00 |\n\nOrder 1001 appears once in `orders` but twice in `order_items`, because it contains two products. Its `total_amount` (78.00) equals 48.00 + 30.00. Keep that \"one order, many lines\" shape in mind: it causes most of the double counting you will see in this course.\n\n## Grain: the most important word in analytics\n\nThe **grain** of a table is what a single row represents. Say it as a sentence: \"`order_items` has one row per order per product.\"\n\n> Before you query a table, state its grain out loud. Every count, sum and join you write depends on it.\n\nIf you misjudge the grain, your numbers break silently. Summing `total_amount` after joining to `order_items` repeats order 1001's 78.00 twice, because the joined result is now at line grain.\n\n## Fact tables and dimension tables\n\nAnalytics warehouses usually split tables into two shapes, an idea popularized by Ralph Kimball's dimensional modeling.\n\n| | Fact table | Dimension table |\n|---|---|---|\n| Describes | events or measurements | the things involved in events |\n| Nestwell examples | `orders`, `order_items`, `web_sessions` | `customers`, `products` |\n| Typical columns | foreign keys, dates, numbers you sum | descriptive attributes you group by |\n| Growth | grows every day | grows slowly |\n\n- You **aggregate** facts: sum revenue, count sessions.\n- You **slice** by dimensions: revenue by `country`, units by `category`.\n- A star schema is a fact table in the middle with dimensions around it. Nestwell's `order_items` joins to `products` (what was bought) and, through `orders`, to `customers` (who bought it).\n\n## Worked example: checking the grain yourself\n\nDo not trust a table's name. Test the grain with a quick count. If the key columns are truly unique, the distinct count equals the row count.\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS row_count,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT (order_id, product_id)) AS distinct_keys\nFROM order_items;\n```\n\n| row_count | distinct_keys |\n|---|---|\n| 21 | 21 |\n\nThe pair is unique, so the grain is confirmed. Counting a pair as `(order_id, product_id)` works in PostgreSQL; in databases that do not support it, use the `GROUP BY` check shown below. Now test the wrong assumption, that `order_items` is one row per order:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS row_count,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) AS distinct_orders\nFROM order_items;\n```\n\n| row_count | distinct_orders |\n|---|---|\n| 21 | 16 |\n\n21 rows but only 16 orders: some orders have several lines. A third pattern finds any offending keys directly:\n\n```sql\nSELECT order_id, product_id, COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM order_items\nGROUP BY order_id, product_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n```\n\nIt returns zero rows, which is exactly what you want. You will use this \"group by the key, keep counts above 1\" check constantly. The `GROUP BY` and `HAVING` syntax is covered in detail in a later module.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Stakeholders ask in business words (\"how many orders?\"). You translate that into a table and a grain, and the wrong grain gives the wrong answer.\n- Knowing facts from dimensions tells you what to sum and what to group by.\n- Grain checks catch duplicate loads and broken pipelines before your numbers reach a dashboard.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Assuming a table's grain from its name. `order_items` sounds like \"orders\" but is one row per product line.\n- Summing an order-level amount after joining to a line-level table, which repeats the amount once per line.\n- Treating a foreign key as unique. `orders.customer_id` repeats for every order the customer places.\n- Skipping the uniqueness check because \"the pipeline guarantees it\". Pipelines fail.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Tables connect through keys: a foreign key points at another table's primary key.\n- Grain is what one row represents. State it before every query.\n- Fact tables record events you aggregate; dimension tables describe things you slice by.\n- Verify grain with `COUNT(*)` versus `COUNT(DISTINCT key)`, or by grouping on the key and looking for counts above 1."
   },
   {
    "title": "SELECT, WHERE and NULL three-valued logic",
    "minutes": 8,
    "body": "Every analysis starts by pulling the right rows and columns. This module covers the core query clauses and the one rule that trips up even experienced analysts: how `NULL` behaves.\n\n## The shape of a query\n\n```sql\nSELECT order_id, customer_id, order_date, total_amount\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed'\n  AND order_date BETWEEN '2026-03-01' AND '2026-03-31'\nORDER BY total_amount DESC\nLIMIT 3;\n```\n\n| order_id | customer_id | order_date | total_amount |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1013 | 2 | 2026-03-18 | 60.00 |\n| 1009 | 3 | 2026-03-01 | 48.00 |\n| 1014 | 5 | 2026-03-25 | 36.00 |\n\n- `SELECT` lists the columns to return. Avoid `SELECT *` in saved queries: it hides what you depend on and breaks when columns change.\n- `FROM` names the table.\n- `WHERE` keeps only rows where the condition is true.\n- `ORDER BY ... DESC` sorts largest first. Without `ORDER BY`, row order is not guaranteed.\n- `LIMIT` caps the rows returned. SQL Server uses `TOP 3` instead; standard SQL also has `FETCH FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY`.\n\nThe database does not run clauses in the order you write them. The logical order is `FROM` -> `WHERE` -> `GROUP BY` -> `HAVING` -> `SELECT` -> `ORDER BY` -> `LIMIT`. That is why a column alias defined in `SELECT` cannot be used in `WHERE`.\n\n## Filtering tools\n\n| Operator | Example | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `=`, `<>`, `<`, `>=` | `total_amount >= 50` | comparison (`<>` means not equal) |\n| `IN` | `country IN ('US', 'CA')` | matches any value in the list |\n| `BETWEEN` | `order_date BETWEEN '2026-03-01' AND '2026-03-31'` | inclusive on both ends |\n| `LIKE` | `name LIKE '%Pan%'` | pattern match; `%` is any characters |\n| `IS NULL` | `channel IS NULL` | the value is missing |\n\n`BETWEEN` is safe on a `DATE` column. On a timestamp column it silently drops most of the last day, because `'2026-03-31'` means midnight. For timestamps use a half-open range: `>= '2026-03-01' AND < '2026-04-01'`.\n\n## AND, OR and parentheses\n\n`AND` binds tighter than `OR`. A manager asks for \"completed or returned orders over 40\":\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed' OR status = 'returned' AND total_amount > 40;\n```\n\nThis returns 14, because SQL reads it as \"completed (any amount) OR (returned and over 40)\". With parentheses, `WHERE (status = 'completed' OR status = 'returned') AND total_amount > 40` returns the intended 7. Always add parentheses when you mix `AND` and `OR`.\n\n## NULL and three-valued logic\n\n`NULL` means \"unknown or missing\". It is not zero and not an empty string. Any comparison with `NULL` returns **unknown** (shown as `NULL`), not true or false.\n\nSQL therefore uses three truth values: true, false and unknown.\n\n| Expression | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `NULL = NULL` | NULL (unknown) |\n| `NULL IS NULL` | true |\n| `1 IN (1, NULL)` | true |\n| `1 NOT IN (2, NULL)` | NULL (unknown) |\n| `NULL AND false` | false |\n| `NULL OR true` | true |\n\n> `WHERE` keeps a row only when the condition is true. Rows where it is unknown are dropped, so any filter on a column with NULLs needs an explicit decision about them.\n\n## Worked example: the missing customer\n\nNestwell has 8 customers. Customer 8 signed up with no tracked channel, so `channel` is `NULL`. Marketing asks: \"How many customers did not come from paid search?\" Two customers did, so the answer should be 6.\n\n```sql\nSELECT customer_id, channel\nFROM customers\nWHERE channel <> 'paid_search'\nORDER BY customer_id;\n```\n\n| customer_id | channel |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | organic |\n| 3 | social |\n| 4 | organic |\n| 6 | referral |\n| 7 | social |\n\nOnly 5 rows. For customer 8, `NULL <> 'paid_search'` is unknown, so the row is filtered out. Three ways to fix it:\n\n- `WHERE channel <> 'paid_search' OR channel IS NULL`\n- `WHERE channel IS DISTINCT FROM 'paid_search'` (treats NULL as a comparable value; returns 6)\n- Replace the NULL first with `COALESCE`, which returns the first non-NULL argument:\n\n```sql\nSELECT customer_id, country, channel,\n       COALESCE(channel, 'unknown') AS channel_clean\nFROM customers\nWHERE country = 'CA';\n```\n\n| customer_id | country | channel | channel_clean |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 | CA | paid_search | paid_search |\n| 8 | CA | NULL | unknown |\n\nLabeling missing values as `'unknown'` is often the best choice for reports, because the gap stays visible instead of vanishing.\n\n## NULLs in counts\n\n`COUNT(*)` counts rows. `COUNT(column)` counts non-NULL values in that column. On `customers`, `COUNT(*)` is 8 and `COUNT(channel)` is 7.\n\nThat difference is a quick way to measure missing data.\n\nAlso watch for NULL foreign keys. In `web_sessions`, sessions 2 and 19 have `customer_id` NULL because the visitor was not logged in. You will see in the joins module how that one NULL breaks `NOT IN`.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Analysts are trusted for exact numbers. A silent NULL drop turns \"6 customers\" into \"5\" with no error message.\n- Attribution, tracking and survey data are full of missing values, so NULL handling is daily work.\n- Clear filters with parentheses make your queries reviewable by teammates.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Writing `= NULL` instead of `IS NULL`. `= NULL` is never true, so the filter returns nothing.\n- Using `<>` or `NOT IN` on a nullable column and silently losing the NULL rows.\n- Mixing `AND` and `OR` without parentheses.\n- Using `BETWEEN` with an end date on a timestamp column and missing most of the final day.\n- Relying on row order without an `ORDER BY`.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Logical order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT.\n- Comparisons with NULL return unknown, and `WHERE` drops unknown rows.\n- Use `IS NULL`, `IS DISTINCT FROM` or `COALESCE` to handle missing values on purpose.\n- `COUNT(*)` counts rows; `COUNT(col)` skips NULLs.\n- Parenthesize mixed `AND`/`OR` and use half-open ranges for timestamps."
   },
   {
    "title": "Aggregation, GROUP BY, HAVING and grain",
    "minutes": 8,
    "body": "Aggregation turns many rows into a summary. It is also where grain changes: the output of a `GROUP BY` has a new grain, one row per group.\n\n## Aggregate functions\n\n| Function | Returns | NULL handling |\n|---|---|---|\n| `COUNT(*)` | number of rows | counts every row |\n| `COUNT(col)` | number of non-NULL values | skips NULLs |\n| `COUNT(DISTINCT col)` | number of different non-NULL values | skips NULLs |\n| `SUM(col)` | total | skips NULLs; NULL if every value is NULL |\n| `AVG(col)` | mean of non-NULL values | skips NULLs, so they do not count as zero |\n| `MIN(col)`, `MAX(col)` | smallest, largest | work on numbers, text and dates |\n\nWithout `GROUP BY`, an aggregate collapses the whole filtered table into one row:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS orders,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS customers,\n       SUM(total_amount) AS revenue,\n       ROUND(AVG(total_amount), 2) AS avg_order_value,\n       MIN(order_date) AS first_order,\n       MAX(order_date) AS last_order\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed';\n```\n\n| orders | customers | revenue | avg_order_value | first_order | last_order |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 14 | 6 | 646.00 | 46.14 | 2026-01-05 | 2026-04-06 |\n\nCheck: 646.00 / 14 = 46.14. Six of the eight customers have a completed order.\n\n## GROUP BY: one row per group\n\n`GROUP BY` splits rows into groups and runs the aggregates once per group.\n\n```sql\nSELECT status, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(total_amount) AS amount\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY status\nORDER BY orders DESC;\n```\n\n| status | orders | amount |\n|---|---|---|\n| completed | 14 | 646.00 |\n| cancelled | 1 | 48.00 |\n| returned | 1 | 35.00 |\n\nThe rule: every column in `SELECT` must either be in `GROUP BY` or be inside an aggregate. Selecting `order_date` here would fail, because each status group has many dates and the database cannot pick one.\n\n> Every column in SELECT is either a grouping key or wrapped in an aggregate. The grouping keys define the grain of your result.\n\n## WHERE vs HAVING\n\n- `WHERE` filters **rows before** grouping.\n- `HAVING` filters **groups after** aggregation, so it can use aggregates.\n\nCustomer success wants Nestwell's most loyal buyers: customers with at least 3 completed orders.\n\n```sql\nSELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 3\nORDER BY revenue DESC;\n```\n\n| customer_id | orders | revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4 | 168.00 |\n| 2 | 3 | 135.00 |\n\nThe `WHERE` removes cancelled and returned orders first, then `HAVING` keeps groups with 3 or more. Put row filters in `WHERE` whenever possible: it is clearer and lets the database discard rows early.\n\n## Worked example: average of what?\n\nFinance asks for \"average order value\". Here is the per-customer view:\n\n| customer_id | orders | revenue | aov |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4 | 168.00 | 42.00 |\n| 2 | 3 | 135.00 | 45.00 |\n| 3 | 1 | 48.00 | 48.00 |\n| 4 | 2 | 94.00 | 47.00 |\n| 5 | 2 | 119.00 | 59.50 |\n| 7 | 2 | 82.00 | 41.00 |\n\n- Order-weighted AOV: 646.00 / 14 orders = **46.14**.\n- Average of the six customer averages: (42.00 + 45.00 + 48.00 + 47.00 + 59.50 + 41.00) / 6 = 282.50 / 6 = **47.08**.\n\nBoth are valid, but they answer different questions. The first is \"what does a typical order bring in?\" and the second is \"what does a typical customer spend per order?\". Averaging averages gives every customer equal weight, regardless of how many orders they placed.\n\nDecide which grain the question is about, and label the metric.\n\n## Grain shifts and line-level tables\n\n`order_items` is at line grain. Summing it gives product-level results:\n\n```sql\nSELECT product_id,\n       SUM(quantity) AS units,\n       SUM(quantity * unit_price) AS item_revenue\nFROM order_items\nGROUP BY product_id\nORDER BY item_revenue DESC;\n```\n\n| product_id | units | item_revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| 101 | 5 | 240.00 |\n| 102 | 5 | 150.00 |\n| 105 | 6 | 126.00 |\n| 104 | 3 | 105.00 |\n| 103 | 6 | 72.00 |\n| 106 | 2 | 36.00 |\n\nThese lines add up to 729.00, not 646.00. `order_items` has no status column, so this includes the cancelled order (48.00) and the returned order (35.00): 646.00 + 48.00 + 35.00 = 729.00.\n\nTo match completed revenue, you need a join to `orders`, covered in the next module. Reconciling two totals like this is a habit worth building early.\n\n## Integer division\n\nIn PostgreSQL and SQL Server, dividing two integers gives an integer. `SELECT 14 / 16` returns 0, not 0.875. Multiply by `1.0` or cast to numeric first: `14 * 1.0 / 16` returns 0.875.\n\nMySQL returns a decimal here, so this is a dialect difference that bites when you move queries between databases.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Most stakeholder questions are aggregates by a dimension: revenue by month, orders by channel.\n- Choosing the right grain for an average or a rate is a judgment call you will defend in reviews.\n- Reconciling totals across tables catches filters you forgot, such as status.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Selecting a column that is neither grouped nor aggregated.\n- Filtering an aggregate in `WHERE` (`WHERE COUNT(*) > 2`) instead of `HAVING`.\n- Averaging pre-computed averages when the question needs a weighted average.\n- Using `COUNT(*)` when you mean `COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id)`.\n- Integer division that silently returns 0 for a rate.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Aggregates collapse rows; `GROUP BY` makes one output row per group.\n- The grouping keys are the grain of your result.\n- `WHERE` filters rows before grouping; `HAVING` filters groups after.\n- An average depends on its grain: order-weighted and customer-weighted AOV differ.\n- Cast to decimal before dividing counts."
   },
   {
    "title": "Joins in depth: fan-out, semi joins and anti joins",
    "minutes": 10,
    "body": "Joins combine tables through their keys. They are also the top source of wrong totals, so this module covers failure modes as much as syntax.\n\n## Join types at a glance\n\n| Join | Keeps |\n|---|---|\n| `INNER JOIN` | rows with a match on both sides |\n| `LEFT JOIN` | every left row, plus matches; NULLs where none |\n| `FULL OUTER JOIN` | every row from both sides; use it to reconcile two lists |\n| Semi join (`EXISTS`) | left rows with at least one match, once each |\n| Anti join (`NOT EXISTS`) | left rows with no match |\n\nCompleted revenue by country needs `orders` (the amount) and `customers` (the country):\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.country, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(o.total_amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders AS o\nINNER JOIN customers AS c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY c.country\nORDER BY revenue DESC;\n```\n\n| country | orders | revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| US | 11 | 479.00 |\n| CA | 2 | 119.00 |\n| UK | 1 | 48.00 |\n\nThe total, 646.00, matches completed revenue from the previous module. Prefix every column with its table alias (`o.`, `c.`) once a query has two tables.\n\n## LEFT JOIN and where the filter goes\n\nTo list completed orders per customer, including customers with none, put the status filter in the `ON` clause:\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.customer_id, COUNT(o.order_id) AS completed_orders\nFROM customers AS c\nLEFT JOIN orders AS o\n  ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\n AND o.status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY c.customer_id\nORDER BY c.customer_id;\n```\n\n| customer_id | completed_orders |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | 4 |\n| 2 | 3 |\n| 3 | 1 |\n| 4 | 2 |\n| 5 | 2 |\n| 6 | 0 |\n| 7 | 2 |\n| 8 | 0 |\n\nMove `o.status = 'completed'` into `WHERE` and customers 6 and 8 disappear: their unmatched rows have `o.status` NULL, and `WHERE` drops unknowns. That quietly turns your LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN. Use `COUNT(o.order_id)`, not `COUNT(*)`, so unmatched customers count as 0, not 1.\n\n## Fan-out: when a join multiplies rows\n\nA join returns one row per matching pair. If a key repeats on both sides, or you join a \"one\" table to a \"many\" table and then sum a \"one\"-level number, totals inflate.\n\n```sql\nSELECT SUM(o.total_amount) AS inflated,\n       SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS line_revenue,\n       COUNT(*) AS rows_after_join\nFROM orders AS o\nJOIN order_items AS oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\nWHERE o.status = 'completed';\n```\n\n| inflated | line_revenue | rows_after_join |\n|---|---|---|\n| 933.00 | 646.00 | 19 |\n\nThe 14 completed orders became 19 rows, because five orders have two lines. Each of those orders' `total_amount` is counted twice: 646.00 + (78 + 40 + 83 + 34 + 52) = 933.00. Summing the line-level amount gives the right 646.00.\n\n> After every join, ask: what is the grain of the result? Only sum a column that lives at that grain, or aggregate each table to a shared grain before joining.\n\nMany-to-many joins are worse. Joining `orders` to `web_sessions` on `customer_id` returns 47 rows from 16 orders and 23 sessions, because customer 1 alone has 4 orders x 4 sessions = 16 rows. Aggregate each side to one row per customer first, then join.\n\n## Semi joins and anti joins\n\nA semi join answers \"who has at least one X?\" without duplicating rows. `EXISTS` stops at the first match:\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.customer_id, c.country\nFROM customers AS c\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o\n  WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id\n    AND o.status = 'completed'\n);\n```\n\nIt returns customers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 7, once each. An inner join to `orders` would return 14 rows, one per order, and need `DISTINCT` to fix.\n\n## Worked example: the NOT IN trap\n\nMarketing wants to target customers who have never visited on mobile, for an app-install campaign. The correct anti join:\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.customer_id, c.country\nFROM customers AS c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM web_sessions AS s\n  WHERE s.customer_id = c.customer_id\n    AND s.device = 'mobile'\n)\nORDER BY c.customer_id;\n```\n\n| customer_id | country |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | US |\n| 4 | US |\n| 5 | CA |\n\nThe tempting `WHERE c.customer_id NOT IN (SELECT s.customer_id FROM web_sessions AS s WHERE s.device = 'mobile')` returns **zero rows**. Anonymous mobile sessions have `customer_id` NULL, so for customer 1 the test becomes `1 NOT IN (2, 3, ..., NULL)`, which is unknown. Prefer `NOT EXISTS`, or a `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right_key IS NULL`, for anti joins.\n\n## FULL OUTER JOIN for reconciliation\n\nFull-joining sessions per customer to orders per customer shows who is on only one side:\n\n| s_customer | o_customer | sessions | orders |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6 | 6 | 2 | 1 |\n| 7 | 7 | 4 | 2 |\n| 8 | NULL | 2 | NULL |\n| NULL | NULL | 2 | NULL |\n\nCustomers 1 to 5 match on both sides and are omitted. Customer 8 browsed but never ordered, and the last row is anonymous sessions: a NULL key never matches anything, not even another NULL.\n\n## Self joins\n\nA self join joins a table to itself with two aliases. Which products are bought together?\n\n```sql\nSELECT a.product_id AS product_a, b.product_id AS product_b,\n       COUNT(*) AS orders_together\nFROM order_items AS a\nJOIN order_items AS b\n  ON b.order_id = a.order_id\n AND a.product_id < b.product_id\nGROUP BY a.product_id, b.product_id;\n```\n\nThe `<` keeps each pair once and stops a product pairing with itself. In the sample, five pairs appear once each, such as 101 with 102 from order 1001.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Most analyses touch several tables, and a silent fan-out can badly overstate revenue.\n- \"Who did X but not Y\" questions (churn lists, campaign targets) are anti joins.\n- Full outer joins help you explain why two reports disagree.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Summing an order-level amount after joining to line items.\n- Filtering the right table in `WHERE` after a LEFT JOIN.\n- Using `NOT IN` against a column that can contain NULL.\n- Joining two \"many\" tables on a shared key without aggregating first.\n- Hiding duplicate rows with `DISTINCT` instead of finding their cause.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Check row counts before and after joins; unexpected growth means fan-out.\n- Use `EXISTS` for \"has any\" and `NOT EXISTS` for \"has none\".\n- Put right-table filters for a LEFT JOIN in `ON`.\n- Aggregate to a common grain before joining two fact tables."
   },
   {
    "title": "Dates, time zones and date spines",
    "minutes": 8,
    "body": "Almost every analytics question has a time component: by month, last 7 days, since signup. Dates look simple, but time zones and missing days cause real reporting errors.\n\n## Date and time types\n\n| Type (PostgreSQL) | Holds | Nestwell column |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DATE` | a calendar day | `orders.order_date`, `customers.signup_date` |\n| `TIMESTAMP` | date and time, no time zone | not used here |\n| `TIMESTAMPTZ` | an exact instant, shown in your session's time zone | `web_sessions.session_start` |\n\nA `TIMESTAMPTZ` stores one absolute moment. When you display it or cast it to a date, PostgreSQL converts it using the session's `TimeZone` setting. The same session can therefore fall on different calendar days depending on that setting.\n\n## Truncating, extracting and date math\n\n`DATE_TRUNC` rounds a date down to the start of a period. It is the standard way to group by week, month or quarter.\n\n```sql\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)::date AS month,\n       COUNT(*) AS orders,\n       SUM(total_amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1;\n```\n\n| month | orders | revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-01-01 | 3 | 137.00 |\n| 2026-02-01 | 4 | 201.00 |\n| 2026-03-01 | 5 | 208.00 |\n| 2026-04-01 | 2 | 100.00 |\n\nThe months add up to 646.00, the completed total. `GROUP BY 1` means \"group by the first selected column\"; it is handy, but naming the expression is clearer in shared code.\n\nOther tools you will use constantly:\n\n- `EXTRACT(DOW FROM order_date)` returns day of week (0 = Sunday). `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM ...)` and `EXTRACT(HOUR FROM ...)` work the same way.\n- `order_date - signup_date` returns whole days between two dates. Order 1016 came 35 days after customer 7 signed up.\n- `DATE '2026-03-09' + 7` returns 2026-03-16. Add an `INTERVAL '1 month'` for calendar months.\n- Month math clamps: `DATE '2026-01-31' + INTERVAL '1 month'` gives 2026-02-28.\n\nDialects differ here. BigQuery writes `DATE_TRUNC(order_date, MONTH)`, SQL Server 2022 added `DATETRUNC(month, order_date)`, and MySQL has no `DATE_TRUNC` at all. The concept is the same everywhere.\n\n## Time zones\n\nNestwell is based in Los Angeles and reports in Pacific time, but `session_start` is logged in UTC. Convert before you take the date:\n\n```sql\nSELECT session_id,\n       session_start,\n       session_start AT TIME ZONE 'America/Los_Angeles' AS la_time,\n       (session_start AT TIME ZONE 'America/Los_Angeles')::date AS la_date\nFROM web_sessions\nWHERE session_id IN (7, 11, 22);\n```\n\n| session_id | session_start | la_time | la_date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | 2026-02-10 04:15:00+00 | 2026-02-09 20:15:00 | 2026-02-09 |\n| 11 | 2026-03-01 02:30:00+00 | 2026-02-28 18:30:00 | 2026-02-28 |\n| 22 | 2026-04-06 03:30:00+00 | 2026-04-05 20:30:00 | 2026-04-05 |\n\n- Session 11 moves into a different **month**: a UTC report counts it in March, a Pacific report in February.\n- Session 7 is offset by 8 hours and session 22 by 7 hours, because daylight saving time started on 8 March 2026. Named zones such as `'America/Los_Angeles'` handle this; fixed offsets such as `'-08:00'` do not.\n- In this sample, 3 of 23 sessions land on a different calendar day in Pacific time than in UTC.\n\n## Filtering time ranges\n\nUse half-open ranges: start inclusive, end exclusive.\n\n```sql\nWHERE session_start >= TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-03-01 00:00 America/Los_Angeles'\n  AND session_start <  TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-04-01 00:00 America/Los_Angeles'\n```\n\nThis catches every instant in March (Pacific) exactly once, with no gaps at 23:59:59 and no overlap with April.\n\n## Date spines with generate_series\n\n`GROUP BY` only returns days that have data. Days with no orders simply vanish, which breaks charts and averages. A **date spine** is a complete list of dates that you LEFT JOIN your facts onto.\n\n> Build reports on a date spine. Missing days are zero-activity days, and they must still appear in the result.\n\n## Worked example: average daily revenue\n\nThe operations lead asks for average daily completed revenue for 1 to 10 March. Grouping the orders table alone shows only two days: 48.00 on 1 March and 64.00 on 9 March. Their average is (48.00 + 64.00) / 2 = **56.00**, which is wrong.\n\n```sql\nSELECT d::date AS day,\n       COALESCE(SUM(o.total_amount), 0) AS revenue\nFROM generate_series('2026-03-01'::date, '2026-03-10'::date,\n                     interval '1 day') AS d\nLEFT JOIN orders AS o\n  ON o.order_date = d::date\n AND o.status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY d\nORDER BY d;\n```\n\n| day | revenue |\n|---|---|\n| 2026-03-01 | 48.00 |\n| 2026-03-02 | 0 |\n| ... | ... |\n| 2026-03-08 | 0 |\n| 2026-03-09 | 64.00 |\n| 2026-03-10 | 0 |\n\nThe spine has all 10 days, so the correct average is 112.00 / 10 = **11.20** per day. `COALESCE` turns the empty days' NULL sums into 0. Note the status filter sits in `ON`, so the LEFT JOIN keeps every day.\n\n`generate_series` is PostgreSQL. BigQuery has `GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY`, and many teams keep a permanent calendar table (one row per day, with week, month and holiday flags) that works in any database.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Monthly and weekly trends are the backbone of business reporting, and a time zone slip moves revenue between months.\n- Averages and moving averages over days are wrong unless empty days are included.\n- Stakeholders compare your numbers with finance and product tools that may use a different time zone; you need to explain differences.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Taking the date of a UTC timestamp when the business reports in local time.\n- Using fixed UTC offsets that ignore daylight saving time.\n- Averaging over only the days that had activity.\n- Filtering timestamps with `BETWEEN ... AND '2026-03-31'`, which stops at midnight on the 31st.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `DATE_TRUNC` groups by period; subtracting dates gives days.\n- Convert `TIMESTAMPTZ` with `AT TIME ZONE` and a named zone before deriving dates.\n- Use half-open ranges (`>=` start, `<` next start) for time filters.\n- LEFT JOIN facts onto a date spine and `COALESCE` empty periods to 0."
   },
   {
    "title": "CASE, conditional aggregation and pivoting",
    "minutes": 7,
    "body": "`CASE` is SQL's if-then-else. Combined with aggregates, it lets one query produce many metrics side by side, which is how most report tables are built.\n\n## CASE expressions\n\n```sql\nSELECT CASE\n         WHEN total_amount >= 60 THEN 'large'\n         WHEN total_amount >= 35 THEN 'medium'\n         ELSE 'small'\n       END AS order_size,\n       COUNT(*) AS orders,\n       SUM(total_amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY MIN(total_amount);\n```\n\n| order_size | orders | revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| small | 4 | 106.00 |\n| medium | 6 | 259.00 |\n| large | 4 | 281.00 |\n\nHow it works:\n\n- Conditions are checked **top to bottom**, and the first true one wins. An order of 60.00 is \"large\" because that branch comes first; you do not need `total_amount < 60` in the second branch.\n- If no branch matches and there is no `ELSE`, the result is NULL. Always decide what the `ELSE` should be.\n- A NULL input fails every `WHEN` comparison and falls to `ELSE`. If NULLs need their own label, test `WHEN x IS NULL` first.\n- `ORDER BY MIN(total_amount)` sorts buckets in a logical order instead of alphabetically.\n\nCheck: 106.00 + 259.00 + 281.00 = 646.00, the completed total. Buckets should always add back to the whole.\n\n## Conditional aggregation\n\nPut the `CASE` **inside** the aggregate to count or sum only some rows, while keeping all rows in the group. This builds several metrics in one pass.\n\n| Pattern | What it computes |\n|---|---|\n| `SUM(CASE WHEN cond THEN amount ELSE 0 END)` | total of amount where cond is true |\n| `COUNT(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1 END)` | rows where cond is true (no ELSE: non-matches are NULL and not counted) |\n| `AVG(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` | share of rows where cond is true |\n| `COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE cond)` | PostgreSQL shorthand for a conditional count |\n\n> To compare subsets side by side, put the condition inside the aggregate, not in WHERE. WHERE removes rows from every metric at once.\n\n## Worked example: completion rate by country\n\nThe operations lead wants orders, completed revenue, problem orders and completion rate per country, in one table.\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.country,\n       COUNT(*) AS orders,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN o.status = 'completed'\n                THEN o.total_amount ELSE 0 END) AS completed_revenue,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE o.status <> 'completed') AS not_completed,\n       ROUND(AVG(CASE WHEN o.status = 'completed'\n                      THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END), 3) AS completion_rate\nFROM orders AS o\nJOIN customers AS c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\nGROUP BY c.country\nORDER BY orders DESC;\n```\n\n| country | orders | completed_revenue | not_completed | completion_rate |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| US | 11 | 479.00 | 0 | 1.000 |\n| UK | 3 | 48.00 | 2 | 0.333 |\n| CA | 2 | 119.00 | 0 | 1.000 |\n\n- The UK has 3 orders: one completed (1009), one cancelled (1004) and one returned (1010), so 1 / 3 = 0.333.\n- A `WHERE o.status = 'completed'` would have made every rate 1.000 and hidden the UK problem.\n- The `1.0` inside the `CASE` avoids integer results. `AVG` of 1s and 0s is the share of 1s.\n- With only 3 UK orders, the rate is fragile. Show the counts next to any rate so readers can judge.\n\n`FILTER` is supported in PostgreSQL and SQLite but not in most other databases. The `CASE` form runs everywhere.\n\n## Pivoting: rows to columns\n\nData usually arrives long (one row per month per category). Reports often want it wide (one column per category). Conditional aggregation does the pivot:\n\n```sql\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', o.order_date)::date AS month,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN p.category = 'bedding'\n                THEN oi.quantity * oi.unit_price ELSE 0 END) AS bedding,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN p.category = 'decor'\n                THEN oi.quantity * oi.unit_price ELSE 0 END) AS decor,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN p.category = 'kitchen'\n                THEN oi.quantity * oi.unit_price ELSE 0 END) AS kitchen,\n       SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS total\nFROM orders AS o\nJOIN order_items AS oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\nJOIN products AS p ON p.product_id = oi.product_id\nWHERE o.status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1;\n```\n\n| month | bedding | decor | kitchen | total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-01-01 | 78.00 | 59.00 | 0 | 137.00 |\n| 2026-02-01 | 108.00 | 35.00 | 58.00 | 201.00 |\n| 2026-03-01 | 78.00 | 48.00 | 82.00 | 208.00 |\n| 2026-04-01 | 78.00 | 0 | 22.00 | 100.00 |\n\n- Revenue comes from `order_items`, at line grain, so the join does not inflate it. The monthly totals match the date module: 137, 201, 208, 100.\n- A `total` column lets you check that the categories add up. If a new category appears, the total exposes it.\n- The column list is fixed in the query. SQL Server, Snowflake and BigQuery offer a `PIVOT` operator, but it usually needs the values listed too. For many or changing categories, keep the data long and pivot in your BI tool.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Stakeholder tables are mostly \"metric by segment\" grids, and conditional aggregation builds them in one readable query.\n- Rates such as completion, return and conversion are conditional averages.\n- Bucketing with `CASE` turns raw numbers into segments people can act on.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Overlapping or out-of-order `WHEN` branches, so values land in the wrong bucket.\n- Forgetting `ELSE`, which leaves NULLs that drop out of later filters and joins.\n- Using `ELSE 0` inside `COUNT`: `COUNT` counts 0 as a value, so every row is counted.\n- Filtering in `WHERE` when you meant to compare subsets side by side.\n- Reporting a rate without the count behind it.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `CASE` returns the first matching branch; without `ELSE` it returns NULL.\n- Conditional aggregation computes several subset metrics in one pass.\n- `AVG(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)` is a rate.\n- Pivot with one `SUM(CASE ...)` per column and add a total to check the result."
   },
   {
    "title": "CTEs and subqueries",
    "minutes": 7,
    "body": "Real analysis questions take several steps: summarize, then join, then summarize again. Subqueries and CTEs let you write those steps inside one query.\n\n## Subqueries: a query inside a query\n\nA subquery is a `SELECT` in parentheses used where a value or a table is expected.\n\n| Kind | Where it goes | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scalar subquery | `SELECT` or `WHERE` | exactly one value |\n| `IN` / `EXISTS` subquery | `WHERE` | a set to test against |\n| Derived table | `FROM` | a table you query further (needs an alias) |\n| Correlated subquery | usually `WHERE` | runs per outer row, referencing it |\n\nScalar example: completed orders above the average completed order value (46.14).\n\n```sql\nSELECT order_id, customer_id, total_amount\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed'\n  AND total_amount > (SELECT AVG(total_amount)\n                      FROM orders\n                      WHERE status = 'completed')\nORDER BY total_amount DESC;\n```\n\nIt returns 7 orders, from 1007 (83.00) down to 1009 and 1015 (48.00 each).\n\n## Correlated subqueries\n\nA **correlated** subquery refers to the outer row, so its result changes per row. Which orders beat that same customer's own average?\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.order_id, o.customer_id, o.total_amount\nFROM orders AS o\nWHERE o.status = 'completed'\n  AND o.total_amount > (\n    SELECT AVG(o2.total_amount)\n    FROM orders AS o2\n    WHERE o2.customer_id = o.customer_id\n      AND o2.status = 'completed'\n  )\nORDER BY o.customer_id, o.order_id;\n```\n\n| order_id | customer_id | total_amount |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1001 | 1 | 78.00 |\n| 1015 | 1 | 48.00 |\n| 1013 | 2 | 60.00 |\n| 1005 | 4 | 60.00 |\n| 1007 | 5 | 83.00 |\n| 1016 | 7 | 52.00 |\n\nCustomer 1's average is 168.00 / 4 = 42.00, so orders 1001 and 1015 qualify. Correlated subqueries are readable for small questions; window functions (next modules) usually do the same job more cleanly.\n\n## CTEs: named steps\n\nA **common table expression** (CTE) defines a named, temporary result with `WITH`, which later steps can query like a table. You write `WITH name AS (query)`, separate further CTEs with commas, and finish with the main `SELECT`.\n\n- Steps read top to bottom, in the order you think.\n- Each step has one clear grain, which you can name in a comment.\n- You can debug a step by temporarily ending the query with `SELECT * FROM step_one`.\n\n> Give each CTE one job and one grain. If you cannot say what one row of a step means, the step is doing too much.\n\n## Worked example: value by acquisition channel\n\nMarketing asks: \"For each signup channel, how many customers do we have, how many became repeat buyers (2+ completed orders), and what is revenue per customer?\" Customers with no orders must count, and the fan-out lesson says to aggregate orders to customer grain before joining.\n\n```sql\nWITH customer_orders AS (\n  -- grain: one row per customer with a completed order\n  SELECT customer_id,\n         COUNT(*) AS completed_orders,\n         SUM(total_amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY customer_id\n),\ncustomer_profile AS (\n  -- grain: one row per customer, all 8 kept\n  SELECT c.customer_id,\n         COALESCE(c.channel, 'unknown') AS channel,\n         COALESCE(co.completed_orders, 0) AS completed_orders,\n         COALESCE(co.revenue, 0) AS revenue\n  FROM customers AS c\n  LEFT JOIN customer_orders AS co ON co.customer_id = c.customer_id\n)\nSELECT channel,\n       COUNT(*) AS customers,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE completed_orders >= 2) AS repeat_buyers,\n       SUM(revenue) AS revenue,\n       ROUND(AVG(revenue), 2) AS revenue_per_customer\nFROM customer_profile\nGROUP BY channel\nORDER BY revenue DESC, channel;\n```\n\n| channel | customers | repeat_buyers | revenue | revenue_per_customer |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| paid_search | 2 | 2 | 287.00 | 143.50 |\n| organic | 2 | 2 | 229.00 | 114.50 |\n| social | 2 | 1 | 130.00 | 65.00 |\n| referral | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0.00 |\n| unknown | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0.00 |\n\nChecks before you share it:\n\n- Customers add up to 8 and revenue to 646.00 (287 + 229 + 130), both matching known totals.\n- `COALESCE(co.revenue, 0)` makes customers 6 and 8 count as zero. Without it, `AVG` would skip them and overstate revenue per customer.\n- With two customers per channel this is an illustration, not a finding. On real data, add the counts and let readers judge sample size.\n\n## CTE or subquery?\n\n- Use a CTE for multi-step logic, or when a step is reused.\n- A short inline subquery is fine for a single value or an `EXISTS` test.\n- Performance is usually similar. Since PostgreSQL 12, a CTE referenced once is inlined into the main query; you can force either behavior with `MATERIALIZED` or `NOT MATERIALIZED`. Other engines have their own rules, so check the plan when it matters.\n- `WITH RECURSIVE` exists for hierarchies such as category trees, but most analytics work does not need it.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Analyst questions are rarely one step; CTEs let you build and check each step.\n- Reviewers can follow a CTE chain line by line, which makes your work trusted and reusable.\n- Aggregating in a CTE before joining is the standard fix for fan-out.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- A scalar subquery that returns more than one row, which raises an error.\n- Forgetting the alias on a derived table in `FROM`.\n- One giant CTE that mixes grains, so nobody can verify it.\n- Losing zero-activity customers by inner joining a summary CTE.\n- Never testing intermediate steps against known totals.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Subqueries can return a value, a set or a table; correlated ones run per outer row.\n- CTEs turn a complex question into named, testable steps.\n- Give each step one grain, and aggregate before joining.\n- Check final totals against numbers you already trust."
   },
   {
    "title": "Window functions: ranking, top-N and dedup",
    "minutes": 9,
    "body": "Window functions compute across a set of related rows without collapsing them. They are the tool analysts reach for most once basic SQL is in place.\n\n## GROUP BY vs window functions\n\n`GROUP BY` returns one row per group. A window function keeps every row and adds a column computed over a \"window\" of rows.\n\n```sql\nSELECT order_id, customer_id, total_amount,\n       SUM(total_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id) AS customer_revenue,\n       ROUND(100.0 * total_amount\n             / SUM(total_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id), 1) AS pct_of_customer\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed' AND customer_id IN (1, 2)\nORDER BY customer_id, order_id;\n```\n\n| order_id | customer_id | total_amount | customer_revenue | pct_of_customer |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1001 | 1 | 78.00 | 168.00 | 46.4 |\n| 1003 | 1 | 24.00 | 168.00 | 14.3 |\n| 1008 | 1 | 18.00 | 168.00 | 10.7 |\n| 1015 | 1 | 48.00 | 168.00 | 28.6 |\n| 1002 | 2 | 35.00 | 135.00 | 25.9 |\n| 1006 | 2 | 40.00 | 135.00 | 29.6 |\n| 1013 | 2 | 60.00 | 135.00 | 44.4 |\n\nThe anatomy of `function() OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)`:\n\n- `PARTITION BY` splits rows into independent groups, like `GROUP BY` but without collapsing. Leave it out and the whole result is one window.\n- `ORDER BY` inside `OVER` sets the order within each partition. Ranking functions require it.\n- Windows run after `WHERE`, `GROUP BY` and `HAVING`, so they see only rows that survived those steps.\n\n## ROW_NUMBER, RANK and DENSE_RANK\n\nThe three ranking functions differ only in how they treat ties. Two completed orders are worth 60.00 and two are worth 48.00:\n\n```sql\nSELECT order_id, total_amount,\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY total_amount DESC, order_id) AS row_num,\n       RANK()       OVER (ORDER BY total_amount DESC) AS rnk,\n       DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total_amount DESC) AS dense_rnk\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed'\nORDER BY total_amount DESC, order_id\nLIMIT 7;\n```\n\n| order_id | total_amount | row_num | rnk | dense_rnk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1007 | 83.00 | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n| 1001 | 78.00 | 2 | 2 | 2 |\n| 1005 | 60.00 | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n| 1013 | 60.00 | 4 | 3 | 3 |\n| 1016 | 52.00 | 5 | 5 | 4 |\n| 1009 | 48.00 | 6 | 6 | 5 |\n| 1015 | 48.00 | 7 | 6 | 5 |\n\n| Function | Ties get | After a tie | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `ROW_NUMBER` | different numbers | continues 1, 2, 3 | exactly one row per group, dedup |\n| `RANK` | the same number | skips (3, 3, 5) | \"top N\" where ties both count |\n| `DENSE_RANK` | the same number | no gap (3, 3, 4) | \"Nth highest distinct value\" |\n\n`ROW_NUMBER` breaks ties arbitrarily unless the `ORDER BY` makes every row unique. Without `order_id` as a tie-breaker, orders 1005 and 1013 can swap positions between runs.\n\n> Make the ORDER BY inside ROW_NUMBER unique, usually by adding the key as a final tie-breaker. Otherwise \"the first row\" can change from one run to the next.\n\n## Top-N per group\n\nYou cannot filter on a window function in `WHERE`, because `WHERE` runs first. Compute the rank in a CTE, then filter outside it.\n\nThe merchandising team asks for the best-selling product in each category by completed revenue:\n\n```sql\nWITH product_revenue AS (\n  SELECT p.category, p.name,\n         SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue\n  FROM order_items AS oi\n  JOIN orders AS o ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\n  JOIN products AS p ON p.product_id = oi.product_id\n  WHERE o.status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY p.category, p.name\n),\nranked AS (\n  SELECT category, name, revenue,\n         RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY revenue DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM product_revenue\n)\nSELECT category, name, revenue\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rnk = 1\nORDER BY revenue DESC;\n```\n\n| category | name | revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| bedding | Linen Duvet Cover | 192.00 |\n| kitchen | Chef Knife | 126.00 |\n| decor | Ceramic Vase | 72.00 |\n\nDecor is close: the vase earned 72.00 and the Wool Throw 70.00. `RANK` would return both if they tied, which is usually what a merchandiser wants. Snowflake, BigQuery and Databricks support `QUALIFY rnk = 1` to filter windows without a CTE; PostgreSQL does not.\n\n## Worked example: deduplicating sessions\n\nA tracking bug sometimes fires the session event twice. Find rows that match on everything except the ID:\n\n```sql\nSELECT customer_id, session_start, device, source,\n       COUNT(*) AS copies, MIN(session_id) AS keep_id\nFROM web_sessions\nGROUP BY customer_id, session_start, device, source\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n```\n\n| customer_id | session_start | device | source | copies | keep_id |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | 2026-03-09 21:30:00+00 | mobile | social | 2 | 15 |\n\nSessions 15 and 16 are the same visit. To keep exactly one row per real visit:\n\n```sql\nWITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT ws.*,\n         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n           PARTITION BY customer_id, session_start, device, source\n           ORDER BY session_id\n         ) AS rn\n  FROM web_sessions AS ws\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS clean_sessions\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1;\n```\n\nThis returns 22 sessions instead of 23. The same pattern gives each customer's first order: `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date, order_id)` and keep `= 1`. Customer 1's orders are numbered 1001 -> 1, 1003 -> 2, 1008 -> 3, 1015 -> 4.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- \"Latest status per order\", \"first purchase per customer\" and \"top 3 per region\" are everyday analyst requests.\n- Raw event data contains duplicates, and dedup with `ROW_NUMBER` is a core cleaning step.\n- Percent-of-group columns let stakeholders see share and total in one table.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Filtering a window result in `WHERE` instead of in an outer query.\n- A non-unique `ORDER BY` in `ROW_NUMBER`, giving unstable results.\n- Using `ROW_NUMBER` for \"top N\" when ties should share a place.\n- Forgetting `PARTITION BY`, so ranks run across all rows instead of per group.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Window functions add a computed column without collapsing rows.\n- `PARTITION BY` sets the groups; `ORDER BY` inside `OVER` sets the sequence.\n- `ROW_NUMBER` for one row per group, `RANK` or `DENSE_RANK` when ties matter.\n- Top-N and dedup: rank in a CTE, then filter on the rank."
   },
   {
    "title": "Window functions: running totals, LAG and frames",
    "minutes": 8,
    "body": "This module covers window calculations that depend on row order: running totals, moving averages and comparisons with the previous row.\n\n## Running totals and LAG\n\nPut an aggregate such as `SUM` over an ordered window and it becomes cumulative. `LAG(col)` returns the value from the previous row in the window, and `LEAD(col)` the next one.\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)::date AS month,\n         SUM(total_amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY 1\n)\nSELECT month, revenue,\n       SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS running_revenue,\n       LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS prev_month,\n       ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n             / LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month), 1) AS mom_growth_pct\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month;\n```\n\n| month | revenue | running_revenue | prev_month | mom_growth_pct |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-01-01 | 137.00 | 137.00 | NULL | NULL |\n| 2026-02-01 | 201.00 | 338.00 | 137.00 | 46.7 |\n| 2026-03-01 | 208.00 | 546.00 | 201.00 | 3.5 |\n| 2026-04-01 | 100.00 | 646.00 | 208.00 | -51.9 |\n\n- The running total ends at 646.00, the completed total. That is a free correctness check.\n- Growth for February: (201 - 137) / 137 = 46.7%.\n- January has no previous row, so `LAG` returns NULL. That is correct: growth from nothing is undefined.\n- April looks like a 51.9% drop, but the sample data ends on 6 April. Never compare a partial period with full ones.\n- `LAG` takes the previous **row**, not the previous month. Build months on a date spine so a month with no orders is not skipped.\n\n## Worked example: time between orders\n\nThe retention team wants the typical gap between repeat orders. `LAG` within each customer gives it:\n\n```sql\nSELECT customer_id, order_id, order_date,\n       LAG(order_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id\n                             ORDER BY order_date, order_id) AS prev_order_date,\n       order_date - LAG(order_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id\n                             ORDER BY order_date, order_id) AS days_since_prev\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed' AND customer_id = 1\nORDER BY order_date;\n```\n\n| customer_id | order_id | order_date | prev_order_date | days_since_prev |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1001 | 2026-01-05 | NULL | NULL |\n| 1 | 1003 | 2026-01-28 | 2026-01-05 | 23 |\n| 1 | 1008 | 2026-02-22 | 2026-01-28 | 25 |\n| 1 | 1015 | 2026-04-02 | 2026-02-22 | 39 |\n\nAcross all customers (without the customer filter) there are 8 repeat gaps: 23, 25, 27, 28, 32, 36, 38 and 39 days. Their mean is 248 / 8 = 31.0 days and their median is (28 + 32) / 2 = 30 days. A re-engagement email around day 30 would be a reasonable test.\n\n## Window frames: ROWS vs RANGE\n\nThe **frame** decides which rows inside the partition feed the calculation.\n\n| Frame clause | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` | this row and the 6 physical rows before it |\n| `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` | from the first row to this one |\n| `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` | up to this row **and all rows tied with it** on the ORDER BY value |\n\nWhen you write `ORDER BY` in `OVER` with no frame, the default is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`. Ties then share one value. Orders 1011 and 1012 both fall on 9 March:\n\n```sql\nSELECT order_id, order_date, total_amount,\n       SUM(total_amount) OVER (ORDER BY order_date) AS range_default,\n       SUM(total_amount) OVER (ORDER BY order_date, order_id\n         ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS rows_running\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed'\n  AND order_date BETWEEN '2026-03-01' AND '2026-03-31'\nORDER BY order_date, order_id;\n```\n\n| order_id | order_date | total_amount | range_default | rows_running |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1009 | 2026-03-01 | 48.00 | 48.00 | 48.00 |\n| 1011 | 2026-03-09 | 34.00 | 112.00 | 82.00 |\n| 1012 | 2026-03-09 | 30.00 | 112.00 | 112.00 |\n| 1013 | 2026-03-18 | 60.00 | 172.00 | 172.00 |\n| 1014 | 2026-03-25 | 36.00 | 208.00 | 208.00 |\n\nRANGE gives \"revenue through the end of this date\"; ROWS gives a true row-by-row running total. Both are valid, for different questions.\n\n> When a window has an ORDER BY, write the frame explicitly. The default RANGE frame groups tied rows and surprises people.\n\n## Moving averages: filter after, not before\n\nA 7-day moving average smooths noisy daily revenue. It needs a date spine, so empty days count as 0, and a `ROWS` frame of the current day plus 6 before it.\n\n```sql\nWITH daily AS (\n  SELECT d::date AS day, COALESCE(SUM(o.total_amount), 0) AS revenue\n  FROM generate_series('2026-02-01'::date, '2026-03-31'::date,\n                       interval '1 day') AS d\n  LEFT JOIN orders AS o\n    ON o.order_date = d::date AND o.status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY d\n),\nsmoothed AS (\n  SELECT day, revenue,\n         ROUND(AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day\n           ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW), 2) AS ma_7d\n  FROM daily\n)\nSELECT day, revenue, ma_7d\nFROM smoothed\nWHERE day BETWEEN '2026-03-07' AND '2026-03-16'\nORDER BY day;\n```\n\n| day | revenue | ma_7d |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-03-07 | 0 | 6.86 |\n| 2026-03-08 | 0 | 0.00 |\n| 2026-03-09 | 64.00 | 9.14 |\n| ... | ... | ... |\n| 2026-03-15 | 0 | 9.14 |\n| 2026-03-16 | 0 | 0.00 |\n\nThe 9 March value is 64.00 / 7 = 9.14. If you put the date filter in the same query as the window, the window only sees the filtered days, and 9 March becomes 64.00 / 3 = 21.33. The window must be computed before you trim the display range.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Running totals, growth rates and smoothed trends appear in nearly every business review.\n- `LAG` turns event tables into durations, such as time between orders.\n- Frame and filtering errors produce plausible-looking wrong numbers.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Relying on the default RANGE frame when dates have ties.\n- Computing moving averages on days with data only, without a spine.\n- Filtering rows before the window, which cuts off the history it needs.\n- Comparing a partial current period with complete earlier ones.\n- Using `LAST_VALUE` with the default frame, which returns the current row's value rather than the partition's last.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `SUM() OVER (ORDER BY ...)` gives running totals; `LAG` and `LEAD` reach neighbor rows.\n- Frames define which rows feed each calculation; write them explicitly.\n- `ROWS` counts physical rows, while `RANGE` includes all ties.\n- Build a spine, compute the window, then filter the output range."
   },
   {
    "title": "Analytical patterns: cohorts, funnels, share of total",
    "minutes": 8,
    "body": "A handful of query patterns answer most recurring business questions. This module assembles earlier tools (CTEs, conditional aggregation, windows) into three of them: share of total, funnels and cohort retention.\n\n## Share of total\n\nAn empty window, `OVER ()`, spans every row in the result. Dividing by it gives each row's share without a second query.\n\n```sql\nWITH category_revenue AS (\n  SELECT p.category, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue\n  FROM order_items AS oi\n  JOIN orders AS o ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\n  JOIN products AS p ON p.product_id = oi.product_id\n  WHERE o.status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY p.category\n)\nSELECT category, revenue,\n       ROUND(100.0 * revenue / SUM(revenue) OVER (), 1) AS pct_of_total\nFROM category_revenue\nORDER BY revenue DESC;\n```\n\n| category | revenue | pct_of_total |\n|---|---|---|\n| bedding | 342.00 | 52.9 |\n| kitchen | 162.00 | 25.1 |\n| decor | 142.00 | 22.0 |\n\nCheck: 342 + 162 + 142 = 646.00, and the shares sum to 100.0. Use `SUM(revenue) OVER (PARTITION BY month)` instead for share within each month.\n\n## Funnels\n\nA **funnel** counts how many units reach each step of a sequence. Define it before you query:\n\n- **Unit**: customers, sessions or orders. Pick one and keep it.\n- **Steps**: each step requires the previous ones.\n- **Window**: the time allowed between steps, if any.\n\nNestwell's customer funnel: signed up -> visited -> placed an order -> completed an order -> became a repeat buyer.\n\n```sql\nWITH customer_steps AS (\n  SELECT c.customer_id,\n         EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM web_sessions AS s\n                 WHERE s.customer_id = c.customer_id) AS visited,\n         EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o\n                 WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id) AS ordered,\n         (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders AS o\n          WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id\n            AND o.status = 'completed') AS completed_orders\n  FROM customers AS c\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS signed_up,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE visited) AS visited,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE visited AND ordered) AS placed_order,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE visited AND completed_orders >= 1) AS completed_order,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE visited AND completed_orders >= 2) AS repeat_buyer\nFROM customer_steps;\n```\n\n| signed_up | visited | placed_order | completed_order | repeat_buyer |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | 8 | 7 | 6 | 5 |\n\n| Step | Customers | Step conversion | From signup |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Signed up | 8 | | 100% |\n| Visited | 8 | 8 / 8 = 100% | 100% |\n| Placed order | 7 | 7 / 8 = 87.5% | 87.5% |\n| Completed order | 6 | 6 / 7 = 85.7% | 75.0% |\n| Repeat buyer | 5 | 5 / 6 = 83.3% | 62.5% |\n\n- The CTE builds one row per customer with a flag per step: the grain is clear and nothing fans out.\n- Each later step includes the earlier conditions (`visited AND ...`), so counts can only fall.\n- Customer 6 placed an order but returned it, which is why \"placed\" and \"completed\" differ.\n\n## Cohort retention\n\nA **cohort** is a group of customers who started in the same period. Retention tracks what share of each cohort is still active N periods later. Here, the cohort is the month of the first completed order and \"active\" means a completed order in that month.\n\n```sql\nWITH first_orders AS (\n  SELECT customer_id,\n         DATE_TRUNC('month', MIN(order_date))::date AS cohort_month\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY customer_id\n),\nactivity AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT o.customer_id, f.cohort_month,\n         (EXTRACT(YEAR FROM o.order_date) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM f.cohort_month)) * 12\n         + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM o.order_date) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM f.cohort_month))\n           AS month_number\n  FROM orders AS o\n  JOIN first_orders AS f ON f.customer_id = o.customer_id\n  WHERE o.status = 'completed'\n)\nSELECT cohort_month,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FILTER (WHERE month_number = 0) AS m0,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FILTER (WHERE month_number = 1) AS m1,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FILTER (WHERE month_number = 2) AS m2,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FILTER (WHERE month_number = 3) AS m3\nFROM activity\nGROUP BY cohort_month\nORDER BY cohort_month;\n```\n\n| cohort_month | m0 | m1 | m2 | m3 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-01-01 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 1 |\n| 2026-02-01 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 0 |\n| 2026-03-01 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n\nHow to read it:\n\n- `m0` is the cohort size: every customer is active in their first month by definition.\n- The January cohort retains 2 / 2 = 100% in month 1 and 1 / 2 = 50% in months 2 and 3.\n- `DISTINCT` in `activity` counts a customer once per month, even with several orders.\n\n> In a cohort table, a period that has not happened yet is not zero retention. Blank out unobserved cells and flag partial periods before anyone reads the table.\n\n## Worked example: reading the cohort table honestly\n\nThe data ends on 6 April 2026. That changes several cells:\n\n- February's `m2` is April, which has only 6 days of data, and its `m3` is May, which has not happened. Both zeros are artifacts.\n- March's `m1` is the partial April, and its `m2` and `m3` are in the future.\n- January's `m3` is also the partial April. Only January `m1` and `m2` and February `m1` are fully observed.\n\nA fair summary: \"Month-1 retention was 100% for the January and February cohorts (2 customers each). Later months are too early or too small to judge.\" Add a `CASE` that returns NULL when `cohort_month + month_number months` is after the last complete month, so the table cannot be misread.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Share of total, funnels and cohorts are the standard lenses for growth, marketing and product questions.\n- Defining the funnel unit and steps up front prevents arguments about whose number is right.\n- Misreading censored cohort cells leads to false \"retention is collapsing\" alarms.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Mixing units in a funnel, such as sessions at the top and customers at the bottom.\n- Steps that do not require earlier steps, so later counts exceed earlier ones.\n- Treating unobserved or partial periods as zero retention.\n- Counting orders instead of distinct customers in a retention cell.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- `x / SUM(x) OVER ()` gives share of total in one query.\n- Build funnels from one row per unit with a flag per step.\n- Cohort tables need a cohort definition, an activity definition and a period index.\n- Always mark incomplete periods before sharing a cohort table."
   },
   {
    "title": "Query performance and reliable SQL",
    "minutes": 7,
    "body": "On 16 sample rows every query is instant and easy to check by eye. On millions of rows you need queries that run fast and results you can prove are right.\n\n## How a query actually runs\n\nYou write SQL that says **what** you want. The database's **optimizer** decides **how**, producing an execution plan. A plan is a tree of steps:\n\n| Plan step | What it does | Fast when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Seq Scan | reads every row of the table | you need most of the table anyway |\n| Index Scan / Bitmap Index Scan | uses an index to jump to matching rows | the filter matches a small share of rows |\n| Hash Join | builds a hash table on one side, probes with the other | joining large sets on equality |\n| Nested Loop | for each outer row, looks up matches | the outer side is small and the inner side is indexed |\n| Aggregate / Sort | groups or orders rows | fewer rows reach it |\n\nThe optimizer chooses based on table **statistics** (row counts, value distributions), which `ANALYZE` refreshes in PostgreSQL.\n\n## Indexes\n\nAn **index** is a separate, sorted structure (usually a B-tree) that lets the database find rows by a column without reading the whole table, like the index at the back of a book.\n\n- Good candidates: columns used in selective `WHERE` filters, join keys and frequent sorts, such as `orders.customer_id` and `orders.order_date`.\n- Costs: extra storage and slower inserts and updates, because each index must be maintained.\n- You may not be allowed to create indexes, but knowing which exist tells you which filters will be fast.\n- Columnar warehouses such as BigQuery, Snowflake and Redshift mostly skip B-tree indexes. They rely on partitioning or clustering and read only the columns you select, so `SELECT *` and unbounded date ranges cost real money.\n\n## Reading EXPLAIN\n\n`EXPLAIN` shows the plan; `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` also runs the query and reports actual rows and time. (Use `ANALYZE` with care on statements that modify data, because it really executes them.)\n\nThe following worked examples use a 2-million-row test copy of `orders` with random data. Timings are from one run on one machine and will differ on yours, and the plans are abridged.\n\n```sql\nEXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT order_id, order_date, total_amount\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = 4217;\n```\n\n```text\nSeq Scan on orders  (actual rows=37)\n  Filter: (customer_id = 4217)\n  Rows Removed by Filter: 1999963\nExecution Time: 138.729 ms\n```\n\nThe database read 2 million rows to keep 37. After `CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);` the same query becomes:\n\n```text\nBitmap Heap Scan on orders  (actual rows=37)\n  ->  Bitmap Index Scan on idx_orders_customer\n        Index Cond: (customer_id = 4217)\nExecution Time: 0.350 ms\n```\n\nLook for scan types on large tables, \"Rows Removed by Filter\" (wasted reads), and big gaps between estimated and actual rows.\n\n## Sargable predicates\n\nA predicate is **sargable** (Search ARGument ABLE) when the database can use an index to satisfy it. Wrapping the indexed column in a function usually breaks that, because the index stores `order_date`, not `DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)`.\n\n> Leave the filtered column bare. Move the logic to the other side of the comparison, usually as a half-open range.\n\n## Worked example: March revenue on 2 million orders\n\nWith an index on `order_date`, compare two filters that return the same 73,419 orders:\n\n| Filter | Plan | Time in test run |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) = DATE '2026-03-01'` | Seq Scan, 1,926,581 rows removed | 463 ms |\n| `order_date >= DATE '2026-03-01' AND order_date < DATE '2026-04-01'` | Bitmap Index Scan on idx_orders_date | 108 ms |\n\nThe function version also made the optimizer estimate 10,000 rows instead of about 74,000, because it cannot use column statistics through a function. Other non-sargable patterns to rewrite:\n\n- `WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM order_date) = 2026` -> `order_date >= '2026-01-01' AND order_date < '2027-01-01'`\n- `WHERE total_amount * 1.1 > 100` -> `WHERE total_amount > 100 / 1.1`\n- `WHERE name LIKE '%Pan'` (leading wildcard) cannot use a standard B-tree index; `LIKE 'Cast%'` can, with the right index setup.\n\n## Write less work for the database\n\n- **Filter early**: put filters in `WHERE` or inside the CTE that reads the big table, not after a join.\n- **Aggregate before joining** two large fact tables, which is also the fan-out fix.\n- **Select only needed columns**, which matters most in columnar warehouses.\n- Use `UNION ALL` unless you need duplicates removed; `UNION` adds a deduplication step.\n- Do not paper over duplicates with `DISTINCT`; it costs a sort or hash and hides a join bug.\n\n## Reliable SQL: test against known totals\n\nA fast query that returns a wrong number is worse than a slow one. Reconcile against a second source:\n\n```sql\nWITH header AS (\n  SELECT SUM(total_amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE status = 'completed'\n),\nlines AS (\n  SELECT SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue\n  FROM order_items AS oi\n  JOIN orders AS o ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\n  WHERE o.status = 'completed'\n)\nSELECT h.revenue AS header_revenue,\n       l.revenue AS line_revenue,\n       h.revenue - l.revenue AS difference\nFROM header AS h\nCROSS JOIN lines AS l;\n```\n\n| header_revenue | line_revenue | difference |\n|---|---|---|\n| 646.00 | 646.00 | 0.00 |\n\nA checklist before you share any number:\n\n1. Grain: does the key you expect to be unique have no duplicates?\n2. Row counts: did any join change the row count unexpectedly?\n3. Totals: do segments add back to the overall total, and does that match a trusted source such as finance's figure?\n4. NULLs: how many rows have NULL in the columns you filter or group by?\n5. Edges: time zone, partial periods, cancelled and returned orders.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Slow queries block shared warehouses and, on pay-per-query platforms, cost money.\n- Reading a plan lets you fix a slow query instead of waiting or guessing.\n- Reconciliation checks are what make stakeholders trust your numbers.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Wrapping indexed columns in functions or casts inside `WHERE`.\n- Assuming an index exists, or that it helps a filter matching most rows.\n- `SELECT *` on wide tables in columnar warehouses.\n- Shipping a number without reconciling it to a known total.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- The optimizer turns your SQL into a plan; `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows it with real rows and time.\n- Indexes speed up selective filters and joins but cost writes and storage.\n- Keep predicates sargable: bare columns, half-open ranges.\n- Filter early, aggregate before joining, select only needed columns.\n- Verify grain, row counts, totals and NULLs before sharing results."
   }
